#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256857 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb183013-a42d-50a3-a1b8-cd8501781075" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Polynomial With Multiple Variables as a Difference of Squares"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ebee3dc9-4221-5947-a670-12da139e9b23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(9{m}^{2}+{n}^{2})(3m+n)(3m-n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebee3dc9-4221-5947-a670-12da139e9b23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(9{m}^{2}+{n}^{2})(3m+n)(3m-n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256859} :answer-field/id #uuid "481a6b4b-d2e2-55f5-b008-269a01e68a2a" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "653b723d-c78b-5196-a990-9f8d17649f57" :question/math-academy-id "q-112044" :question/problem "Factor the expression $81{m}^{4}-{n}^{4}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The difference of squares formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-{b}^{2}=(a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nNotice that our expression can be written as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n81{m}^{4}-{n}^{4}={(9{m}^{2})}^{2}-{({n}^{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{(9{m}^{2})}^{2}-{({n}^{2})}^{2}=(9{m}^{2}+{n}^{2})(9{m}^{2}-{n}^{2})\n$$\n\nFinally, we notice that the expression in the second set of parentheses is also a difference of squares: \n\n$$\n9{m}^{2}-{n}^{2}={(3m)}^{2}-{n}^{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression can be factored further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(9{m}^{2}+{n}^{2})(9{m}^{2}-{n}^{2}) & =(9{m}^{2}+{n}^{2})(3m+n)(3m-n)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257116 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fed1761-ceeb-5033-afa2-cb735e6bb96c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f74fd72-dd28-524a-9c7f-1084f8649375" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257118}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f74fd72-dd28-524a-9c7f-1084f8649375" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257118} :answer-field/id #uuid "d452168e-bfbc-5305-927c-94998c46c6d4" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257117}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1bd10278-9b76-5fcf-980b-136026be1670" :question/math-academy-id "q-114961" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d624edaffd8c90de40041d419cf65bcf8f3af5d31c8d88d1123bd8f3d7555166.png)\n\nWhat is the length of the major axis of the ellipse given above?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The major axis is the longest diameter of the ellipse, which is the line segment bounded by the points $(-3,1)$ and $(5,1).$ So, the length of the major axis is $5-(-3)=8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29e2083d-fe76-5a35-9c3a-7e6ad19ff968" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola With Non-Origin Vertex Passing Through a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d26e455a-be78-54a6-8ee0-868b455d0058" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac34(x-2)^2+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257136}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d26e455a-be78-54a6-8ee0-868b455d0058" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac34(x-2)^2+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257136} :answer-field/id #uuid "77a1bb95-efd6-5816-8d00-b1ae1239d5ad" :answer-field/key "y" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6c976869-dd96-5219-84a7-962a7edc295e" :question/math-academy-id "q-115511" :question/problem "Find the equation of the downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(2,4)$ and which passes through the point $(4,1).$\n\n$y =$: {{y}}" :question/worked-solution "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(2,4).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(4,1),$ so we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=1$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-4) \\\\ (4-2)^{2} & =4p(1-4) \\\\ 4 & =-12p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-4) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{3})(y-4) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =-\\frac{4}{3}(y-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257386 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b05f4be1-95d4-5137-bbaa-71c95862bb59" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions by Factoring an Alternate Form of the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21e66950-5962-5b82-a7a0-6b569173657e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21e66950-5962-5b82-a7a0-6b569173657e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257388} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0c2566c-62b1-5822-b528-94b1c53921a7" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5a918518-91f1-50bb-bef6-5c2c5dd04e5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-119865" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{{sin}^{2}x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}x}-1$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the numerator of the first term using $1-{cos}^{2}x={sin}^{2}x.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sin}^{2}x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}x}-1=\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}x}-1\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the numerator of the first term as a difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}x}-1 & =\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{cos}x)(1+\\operatorname{cos}x)}{1-\\operatorname{cos}x}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(1-\\operatorname{cos}x)}(1+\\operatorname{cos}x)}{\\cancel{1-\\operatorname{cos}x}}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}x}{1}-1 \\\\  & =1+\\operatorname{cos}x-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257802 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed7286fe-b6e2-5544-8bc6-86afc6cdb813" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Matrix by Its Description"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91cbf96f-9d5a-5942-afcf-44040736fccc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257804}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91cbf96f-9d5a-5942-afcf-44040736fccc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257804} :answer-field/id #uuid "a38e78ed-df9c-58d1-90b8-7b6526913134" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "11d30a39-1dc6-581f-aa48-ba6b816cb779" :question/math-academy-id "q-125550" :question/problem "What is a $3\\times 2$ matrix?\n\n$$\nE=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,F=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 8 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,G=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $E$ has $2$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $2\\times 3.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $F$ has $3$ rows and $2$ columns. So, its dimensions are $3\\times 2.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- The matrix $G$ has $3$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $3\\times 3.$ $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, only $F$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258090 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b12a7b7-1df3-5203-934e-8820604de3de" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Radians Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f515b11-ff1f-54cd-bc3a-d7acaa75d91c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}x-\\operatorname{sin}x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f515b11-ff1f-54cd-bc3a-d7acaa75d91c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}x-\\operatorname{sin}x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258092} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e30f645-54b0-5eae-b57e-34f9c4ffffff" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4878f13e-2679-5b9e-8cae-9094f9bb2d55" :question/math-academy-id "q-141281" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}u\\operatorname{cos}v+\\operatorname{cos}u\\operatorname{sin}v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}x-\\operatorname{sin}x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258842 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1671fb4f-937f-5a3c-85b7-7f54f35ea543" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Increasing Using Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bd9bb88-515f-53cf-9423-91c74e4a8660" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258844}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2bd9bb88-515f-53cf-9423-91c74e4a8660" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258844} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2b9ab03-2c43-56b7-a9dc-b74729a8471a" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "20089cbf-a0a2-56b3-8290-b7c3572f1443" :question/math-academy-id "q-21026" :question/problem "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+8$ is strictly increasing.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The function is strictly increasing where ${f}^{'}(x)>0.$ Differentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =2\\cdot 3{x}^{2}-3\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-6x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-6x & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above inequality factors as\n\n$$\n6x(x-1)>0.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=6x(x-1)$ has roots at $x=0$ and $x=1,$ as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/ec7ea6baa82d60a6cf472c904460190042d889d86cb092b19d9809111bd93b36.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the function is strictly increasing on the intervals $x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260204 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4014376-4dae-56f1-b981-b35d3afab874" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Constant Such that the Limit of a Piecewise Function Exists"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3627d8f3-7507-59fd-add7-0a30a7f9cd5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3627d8f3-7507-59fd-add7-0a30a7f9cd5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260206} :answer-field/id #uuid "fef77817-3574-5917-8751-f6dc6ac2eaef" :answer-field/key "b" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260205} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fda01204-9e15-5935-8160-c3810f97cd32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260208}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fda01204-9e15-5935-8160-c3810f97cd32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260208} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e135889-0014-59ef-a4ff-b04ac2c9f0e6" :answer-field/key "c" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260207}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3cbc081a-4656-5dca-b3b1-aa3f8536f992" :question/math-academy-id "q-36743" :question/problem "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x+b\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le 1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+cx+2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nit is known that ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=2.$ What is the value of the constants $b$ and $c?$\n\n$b$: {{b}}\n\n$c$: {{c}}" :question/worked-solution "In order for ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)$ to be equal to $2,$ the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal to $2.$\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={x}^{2}+2x+b.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}({x}^{2}+2x+b) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}+2(1)+b \\\\  & =1+2+b \\\\  & =b+3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left-sided limit must be equal to $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =2 \\\\ b+3 & =2 \\\\ b & =2-3 \\\\ b & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={x}^{2}+cx+2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}({x}^{2}+cx+2) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}+c(1)+2 \\\\  & =1+c+2 \\\\  & =c+3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the right-sided limit must be equal to $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =2 \\\\ c+3 & =2 \\\\ c & =2-3 \\\\ c & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260869 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "831f9aa0-099e-5e0c-8a9d-c4828b629f64" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Radians Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "641d7778-b4a5-5a1f-9803-f84355fa85f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "641d7778-b4a5-5a1f-9803-f84355fa85f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260871} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cfbbf58-a0ba-5f08-ad19-4b0db32db4a1" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8bb03c0c-2bf3-5dda-8102-58698d5cc17e" :question/math-academy-id "q-65502" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}u\\operatorname{cos}v-\\operatorname{sin}u\\operatorname{sin}v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})-\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261501 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11263ba1-2ef9-5c93-8c56-d09dcbea8f8b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum of a Geometric Series Given Two Consecutive Terms of the Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4264b569-e7c0-555a-8ec9-c5a63046db75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "127" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4264b569-e7c0-555a-8ec9-c5a63046db75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "127" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261503} :answer-field/id #uuid "4738a03a-d5cd-5418-9836-3ec26a7ff4bd" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261502}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a1680abc-bbec-518f-a962-a951e4463528" :question/math-academy-id "q-73306" :question/problem "The fifth term of a geometric series is $4$ and the sixth term is $2.$ What is the sum of the first $7$ terms?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term of the series, and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nTo find $r,$ we find the ratio between the consecutive terms ${a}_{5}$ and ${a}_{6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{{a}_{6}}{{a}_{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we must determine the value of ${a}_{1}.$ To do this, we use the formula for the $n$th term, which states that\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=5,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{5} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5-1} \\\\ 4 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4} \\\\ 4 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot \\frac{1}{16} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{4}{(\\frac{1}{16})} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, if we substitute ${a}_{1}=64,N=7$ and $r=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the formula for ${S}_{N},$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{7} & =64\\cdot \\frac{1-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{7}}{1-\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =64\\cdot \\frac{1-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{7}}{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{(\\frac{1}{2})}\\cdot (1-\\frac{1}{128}) \\\\  & =128(1-\\frac{1}{128}) \\\\  & =128-1 \\\\  & =127\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261529 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed3b6c3d-b840-50ef-9e6b-584bf14877b3" :knowledge-point/title "More Complex Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b63163a-78d3-58ce-a7d3-9357d0d7ff1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{{2}^{z}-9}}{\\operatorname{ln}2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b63163a-78d3-58ce-a7d3-9357d0d7ff1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{{2}^{z}-9}}{\\operatorname{ln}2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261531} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad4299e2-f023-5b27-b24b-4bcce5df6b88" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261530}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cf6e7469-a047-53fb-9915-fd6e8f55046c" :question/math-academy-id "q-73670" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{{2}^{z}}{\\sqrt{{2}^{z}-9}}\\,\\text{d}z.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's introduce the new variable $u={2}^{z}-9.$ Differentiating gives \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}={2}^{z}\\operatorname{ln}2\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}2}\\,\\text{d}u={2}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{2}^{z}}{\\sqrt{{2}^{z}-9}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{2}^{z}-9}}\\cdot {2}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}2}\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}2}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{1/2}}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{{2}^{z}-9}}{\\operatorname{ln}2}+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261704 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f783fe8-8f52-5f93-adef-bf4bd030fea0" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Equation of a Vertical Hyperbola in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa18e828-2032-5e08-b585-b038a583bdb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y^2}{10}-\\frac{x^2}{8}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261706}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa18e828-2032-5e08-b585-b038a583bdb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y^2}{10}-\\frac{x^2}{8}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261706} :answer-field/id #uuid "417ff34e-05a0-579a-a5a1-7bcf45081217" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fa7228ce-645a-52a8-9a40-e535c79572db" :question/math-academy-id "q-78257" :question/problem "Write the equation of the hyperbola $4{y}^{2}-5{x}^{2}=40$ in standard form.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, to write the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to divide both sides of the equation by $40$ and then simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2}-5{x}^{2} & =40 \\\\ \\frac{4{y}^{2}}{40}-\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{40} & =\\frac{40}{40} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{10}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
